1: # P3K.34. Proved: the OS49 component line, both signs, and every fan
2: 
3: Root independently passed the complete proof, and blind_control independently
4: passed the signed boundary and normal comparison in Sections 2--4. The input is the actual positive-diagonal OS49 family of P3F.31--32, with
5: `lambda=r^m`, `m=3k`, `k>0`, cap vector
6: `n(1,-1,0,0)+d delta`, `n=+1` or `-1`, and arbitrary integral `d`.
7: We retain the component-supported line of multidegree `(-1,+1)` on the
8: source `I_2`. No whole-fibre height substitution is made.
9: 
10: In the initial positive-diagonal Cox product we obtain
11: \[
12:  k_{\rm Jin}=-6k,\qquad \ell_{\rm Jin}=-24k,\qquad
13:  (m_{\rm rel},n_{\rm rel},s)=(6k,0,0).
14:  \tag{OC.1}
15: \]
16: Both norm signs have the same pair for the positive physical `r`-loop.
17: For an actual allowed fan `T(v,w)`, write the physical delta cocharacter
18: as `delta=a v+b w` and put `S=a^2-ab+b^2`. The resulting return is
19: \[
20:  [\Sigma_{\Phi,T}]
21:        =\frac{3k}{4}(S-1)(S-3)\pmod {28}.
22:  \tag{OC.2}
23: \]
24: All details establishing the signed initial product, rather than only its
25: zero index, are given below.
26: 
27: ## 1. Literal origins and the simultaneous bounded Cox product
28: 
29: We use P3F.32's actual periods, not a relabelled scalar parameter:
30: \[
31:  u=(2s,s)^t+U(q),\quad
32:  \gamma=(-3s,-s+mv)^t+C(q),\quad D=3U+2C,
33: \]
34: \[
35:  H=-u-\gamma,\qquad K=3u+2\gamma=D+(s+2mv)\delta,
36:  \qquad \lambda=r^m.
37:  \tag{OC.3}
38: \]
39: Here `s=log q/(2 pi i)` and `v=log r/(2 pi i)`. In the actual physical
40: cocharacter lattice the tropical vectors of `H,K` are `(1,0),(0,1)`.
41: The period lattice generated by `(2,1),(-3,-1)` has determinant one.
42: On the auxiliary `2m`-root the two actual origins are
43: \[
44:  \sigma=nvD+nmv(v-1)\delta,\qquad
45:  o=-\frac{nsD}{2m}-\frac{ns^2}{4m}\delta-nsv\delta.
46: \]
47: Their differences are exactly
48: \[
49:  (\sigma-o)(v+\theta)- (\sigma-o)(v)
50:        =n\theta K+nm\theta(\theta-1)\delta,
51: \]
52: \[
53:  (\sigma-o)(s+2m,v)- (\sigma-o)(s,v)=nK+nm\delta.
54:  \tag{OC.4}
55: \]
56: The ordinary closing is multiplication by `t^n=q^{-n}`, and
57: `sigma(v+1)-sigma(v)=nK-ns delta`; hence the actual ordinary endpoint
58: minus the cusp-origin endpoint is the integral period `-nK`. This
59: retains the supplied source-root descent and all endpoint scalar factors.
60: 
61: Put `U_K=exp(2 pi i D_1)` and `W_K=exp(2 pi i D_2)`. At an initial
62: parameter `lambda_0=r_0^m`, the bounded angular map determined by this
63: origin is literally
64: \[
65:  C_\theta:\quad
66:  (Z,V)\longmapsto
67:  \left(U_K^{n\theta}Z,
68:        (W_K\lambda_0^2)^{n\theta}
69:        e^{2\pi i nm\theta(\theta-1)}V\right).
70:  \tag{OC.5}
71: \]
72: All powers use the actual logarithms of the nonzero units. At the
73: endpoint this is the bounded `nK` period after reindexing the cell by its
74: integral vertical shift. The scalar quadratic factor is the actual
75: physical delta action. We keep it as a circle gauge; in the orbit normal
76: three-plane it acts identically.
77: 
78: For clarity, this product closes on all three curve charts at once.
79: In the four-coordinate Cox model of K17, after the period-cell reindexing,
80: the return has multipliers
81: \[
82:  (z_0,z_4,z_1,z_2)\longmapsto
83:  ((ab\lambda_0^{2n})^{-1}z_0,z_4,az_1,
84:                      b\lambda_0^{2n}z_2),
85:  \tag{OC.6}
86: \]
87: where `a,b` are the retained signed bounded period units. The product
88: of the four displayed coordinate multipliers is one, so the same map fixes `q=z_0z_4z_1z_2` in every chart. The
89: period lattice acts freely and transitively on cells. Thus no residual
90: cell permutation or finite stabilizer is hidden in (OC.6). The same
91: interval of multipliers as in (OC.5), including any odd closed Cox loop,
92: is used on the two triple charts and all three curve tubes. The finite
93: cap's additional character `exp(2 pi i d/m)` is fixed in `r`; it adds no
94: parameter difference and remains part of the line gluing in Section 5.
95: 
96: ## 2. The mixed boundary coordinates and the exact Thom sign
97: 
98: We distinguish a cycle from its point angle. Let `(x,y)` be the point
99: angles in the ordered cycles `(u,gamma)`, and `(h,kappa)` those in
100: `(H,K)`. Equation (OC.3) gives the actual torus isomorphism
101: \[
102:  \binom h\kappa=
103:  \begin{pmatrix}2&-3\\1&-1\end{pmatrix}\binom xy,
104:  \qquad
105:  \binom xy=
106:  \begin{pmatrix}-1&3\\-1&2\end{pmatrix}\binom h\kappa.
107:  \tag{OC.7}
108: \]
109: The other source cycle is `w`; it is unchanged. These are pointwise
110: torus maps, with unimodular matrices, in the actual incoming collar.
111: 
112: The marked finite-cap map of the signed page calculation applies to
113: this cap without changing its elliptic or line action. Its norm is
114: `m y-n alpha`, where `alpha` is the finite base meridian. The explicit
115: solid-torus map (SCP.1) therefore gives the positive weighted Hopf
116: angle `beta=n y`. Its sheet-zero graph is the actual holomorphic root
117: slice for either sign, so the page has its complex orientation and
118: boundary orientation `or(E_u) d beta`.
119: 
120: Let the incoming compression graph supplied by F31 be
121: `kappa=g(h,w)`, where `g` is a globally real function. The actual positive
122: parameter loop translates this graph by `n theta` in the K direction.
123: Its inverse angular coordinate is
124: \[
125:  \theta=n(\kappa-g(h,w)).
126:  \tag{OC.8}
127: \]
128: Combining (OC.7) with `y=n beta`, the boundary map is the linear torus
129: isomorphism
130: \[
131:  \binom h\theta=
132:  \begin{pmatrix}2&-3n\\ n&-1\end{pmatrix}\binom x\beta
133:                   -\binom0{n g(h,w)}.
134:  \tag{OC.9}
135: \]
136: Its determinant is `-2+3n^2=1`, for both signs; the graph shear also
137: has determinant one. The `u` and `H` source orientations agree with
138: their complex source orientations: their Tate imaginary periods have
139: leading terms `2 Im(s)` and `Im(s)`, respectively, both positive, and
140: the retained bounded units cannot reverse this sign on the chosen
141: small collar. Keeping `w` first, (OC.9) proves the literal orientation
142: identity
143: \[
144:  \partial V_m\longrightarrow E_H\times S^1_\theta
145:                   \quad\hbox{has degree }+1
146:  \tag{OC.10}
147: \]
148: for `n=+1` and `n=-1` separately.
149: 
150: Orient the actual real compression by `partial W=F-E_H`. Then the
151: oriented product `W x S1_theta` attaches to the complex graph page and
152: gives `partial X=F x S1_theta`. In the fixed normal chart the delta
153: weights are `(-1,+1)`. The direct Hopf computation below gives
154: `or(Y)=or(F) or(nu_Hopf)`. At the outgoing trace the tangent orientation
155: is `or(F) dr dtheta`; moving the three normal vectors past these two
156: parameter vectors has positive sign. Hence the normal meridian has
157: the positive physical Hopf orientation for both norms, and the actual
158: Euler line satisfies `pi_* x=+1`. The two bordism boundary reversals
159: and Jin's inverse mapping-torus convention are exactly the literal
160: ones of K7. The complex page and its characteristic numbers therefore
161: enter with the same sign in both cases.
162: 
163: This calculation is needed even though F32's index is zero. We do not
164: use multiplication of that zero by `n` to determine either integer.
165: 
166: ## 3. The decreasing K height and the initial Cox normal frame
167: 
168: At the fixed vertical edge the actual positive-fan charts are
169: \[
170:  (A,B,Z)=(q/V,V/Z,Z),\qquad
171:  (A',B',Z')=(qZ/V,V,1/Z),
172: \]
173: \[
174:  A'=ZA,\quad B'=ZB,\quad Z'=Z^{-1},\qquad q=ZAB.
175:  \tag{OC.11}
176: \]
177: The physical delta action has weights `(-1,+1,0)`. Put
178: `y=AB` and `b=(|B|^2-|A|^2)/2`. Its exact quotient on the two moving
179: coordinates is `(y,b)`. On the regular collar increasing `log|V|`
180: increases `b`, whereas positive `K` multiplies `V` by
181: `q lambda_0^2 W_K`, whose modulus is less than one. Thus the proper
182: K-height `chi_K` has `d chi_K/db<0`. The actual relative orbit product
183: of F31 is chosen with this collar marking. Its two local orbit
184: coordinates have positive transition derivative, as in F31/F9, so the
185: decreasing choice extends through both nodal charts. We retain this
186: negative derivative.
187: 
188: The initial global quaternion normal coordinate is
189: \[
190:  q_H=(1+jZ)(A+j\overline B)
191:      =(Z^{-1}+j)(A'+j\overline {B'}).
192:  \tag{OC.12}
193: \]
194: The equality follows by multiplication using `zj=j conjugate(z)`.
195: At `Z=0`, the map from the complex normal plane `(A,B)` to
196: `A+j conjugate(B)` preserves real four-dimensional orientation.
197: Physical delta is right multiplication by `exp(-i theta)`. Its
198: positive Hopf normal coordinate is `eta=q_H i conjugate(q_H)`.
199: At `Z=0` this is explicitly
200: \[
201:  \eta=-2b\,i+2\operatorname{Im}(y)j-2\operatorname{Re}(y)k.
202:  \tag{OC.13}
203: \]
204: The determinant from `(Re y,Im y,b)` to `(eta_i,eta_j,eta_k)` is `-8`.
205: In the circle-first convention, a direct evaluation at `A=1,B=0`
206: has determinant `-1` for the ordered coordinates
207: `(delta,Z_re,Z_im,y_re,y_im,b)` relative to the complex orientation.
208: The two signs give exactly `or(Y)=or(F) or(eta)`. These equations
209: also identify the orientation at every point by the actual global
210: quaternion coordinate (OC.12).
211: 
212: Use the oriented raw normal coordinates `(Re y,Im y,-b)` at the
213: initial chart. Their change to (OC.13) has positive determinant.
214: Modulo the fixed-curve tangent and its graph shear, the actual
215: K-product derivative in these coordinates is
216: \[
217:  Q_K^+=\begin{pmatrix}
218:  1&0&0\\0&1&0\\
219:  (\chi_K)_{\Re y}&(\chi_K)_{\Im y}&-(\chi_K)_b
220:  \end{pmatrix},\qquad-(\chi_K)_b>0.
221:  \tag{OC.14}
222: \]
223: The source shears contract linearly and the positive last entry
224: contracts to one. The splittings of the intrinsic K normal line and
225: the source normal plane form a contractible space, so these
226: contractions agree on the two charts. They are independent of the
227: parameter angle. This supplies the initial Cox-to-product normal
228: isomorphism with its actual reflection retained at both endpoints.
229: 
230: For the signed rotating coordinate `n kappa`, the raw derivative is
231: `T_n Q_K^+`, `T_n=diag(1,1,n)`. We apply the same constant coordinate
232: change `T_n` to both initial normal frames. Its remaining relative
233: comparison is `T_n^{-1}(T_n Q_K^+)=Q_K^+`. Equivalently its conjugation
234: on a full clutch is conjugation by `n T_n`, an element of `SO(3)`.
235: Thus neither the decreasing K-height nor `n=-1` supplies an additional
236: free three-dimensional framing term.
237: 
238: We also retain the normal derivative of the actual bounded map (OC.5).
239: A unitary torus multiplier `(Z,V)->(aZ,bV)` acts in (OC.11) by
240: `(Z,A,B)->(aZ,pA,q_1B)` with `p=b^{-1}`, `q_1=b/a`, and `a p q_1=1`.
241: Choose continuous roots along its given parameter interval,
242: `L^2=p q_1`, `R^2=p/q_1`, `LR=p`. Direct substitution gives
243: \[
244:  q_H\longmapsto L q_H R,\qquad
245:  \eta\longmapsto L\eta\overline L.
246:  \tag{OC.15}
247: \]
248: The latter rotation is constant along the fixed sphere. Positive real
249: factors are the same normal isomorphism in the two products; they
250: cancel in the relative comparison. Higher terms of the retained base
251: units have zero normal linear part because `q=ZAB` is quadratic in
252: the normal coordinates. Consequently the remaining closed rotation
253: loop, including an odd one, extends over the fixed three-disc. The
254: actual simultaneous Cox product has the same free normal-framing
255: class as the rotating product. No endpoint loop has been replaced by
256: a chosen trivial loop.
257: 
258: ## 4. The degree-2m graph phase and the full normal clutch
259: 
260: The actual one-cap weighted page has regular-binding polynomial
261: `z_2-t_* z_1^m`; its source base is `t=z_2/z_1^m`.
262: Thus in the actual holomorphic binding chart `q_b=t-t_*`, after
263: retaining its fixed phase, the page equation is
264: `alpha=phi-m beta`. If the cusp coordinate is `q=1/t`, this same
265: chart is `q_b=-(q-q_*)/(q q_*)`, a specified holomorphic unit times
266: `q-q_*`; it has nonzero derivative. We use this exact chart throughout
267: the collar calculation. F31's norm-shell interpolation keeps the base
268: coordinate fixed and hence preserves this equation.
269: 
270: On the rotating incoming graph, (OC.7)--(OC.8) give
271: \[
272:  \beta=n(-h+2\kappa)=2\theta+g_n(h,w),\qquad
273:  g_n=n(2g-h),
274: \]
275: \[
276:  \alpha=\phi-2m\theta-m g_n(h,w).
277:  \tag{OC.16}
278: \]
279: The function `g_n` is circle-valued; its `-n h` term is retained.
280: Since `m` is integral, `R_z(-m g_n)` is a global map. The factor two
281: is forced by the actual mixed point-angle map, and is not a further
282: parameter cover.
283: 
284: Use the relative angle
285: `eta_rel=n(kappa-g(h,w))-theta`, which vanishes on the graph.
286: After quotienting by the source tangent, its two parameter Cartesian
287: columns have the same relative coefficients `(-sin theta,cos theta)`
288: as in K30. The derivatives of `g` are precisely the removed triangular
289: source shears. Give the graph its oriented page-normal vector and the
290: Cartesian parameter frame. The common outgoing page and incoming
291: compression collar has tangent
292: `(cos alpha,sin alpha,rho,0)`, `rho>0`, in the binding-plane,
293: parameter-radius, relative-angle quotient. The exact quotient map is
294: \[
295:  (v_q,v_r,v_\eta)\longmapsto
296:  \left(v_q-\frac{v_r}{\rho}(\cos\alpha,\sin\alpha),v_\eta\right).
297:  \tag{OC.17}
298: \]
299: Projecting the two Cartesian parameter vectors and retaining the same
300: positive reference rescalings gives the actual matrix
301: \[
302:  G(\alpha,\theta)=
303:  \begin{pmatrix}
304:  -\sin\alpha&\cos\theta\cos\alpha&\sin\theta\cos\alpha\\
305:  \cos\alpha&\cos\theta\sin\alpha&\sin\theta\sin\alpha\\
306:  0&\sin\theta&-\cos\theta
307:  \end{pmatrix}\in SO(3).
308:  \tag{OC.18}
309: \]
310: The constant signed/reflected coordinates in Section 3 are applied
311: at both ends. Equation (OC.10), followed by the local calculation
312: (OC.13), fixes the positive Hopf normal orientation of (OC.18).
313: 
314: Choose a normal frame on the compression at parameter zero and
315: transport it by the actual K flow with speed `n`. Its difference at
316: the fixed sphere from (OC.12) is a map `S2->SO(3)` and extends over
317: the fixed three-disc. The construction is possible because the
318: compression is a solid torus minus a ball, its tangent bundle is
319: trivial, and the actual `Y=S5` is stably parallelizable; the oriented
320: rank-three normal bundle has zero `w_2` and hence admits a frame on
321: this three-dimensional body. In the transported product the frame is
322: `R(p)`, independent of `theta`.
323: 
324: Since `G(alpha,theta)=R_z(alpha)G(0,theta)`, the full actual clutch is
325: homotopic, by the explicit shear and positive collar homotopies, to
326: \[
327:  \begin{split}
328:  R(p)^{-1}G(\phi-2m\theta-mg_n(p),\theta)
329:    ={}&[R(p)^{-1}R_z(-mg_n(p))]\\
330:      &\cdot[R_z(\phi-2m\theta)G(0,\theta)].
331:  \end{split}
332:  \tag{OC.19}
333: \]
334: The first factor comes from the actual source `T2`, the second from
335: the physical parameter circle. They are globally defined even when
336: the graph has winding or the frame has a nontrivial one-dimensional
337: class. For the product of two such maps, the Maurer--Cartan cubic
338: trace form is the sum of the factor cubic forms plus an exact form;
339: this follows by expanding `(ab)^{-1}d(ab)` and using the two
340: Maurer--Cartan equations. Each factor cubic form vanishes by
341: dimension. The interpolating connection on the clutch collar
342: therefore gives
343: \[
344:              \langle p_1(\overline\nu),[\overline X]\rangle=0.
345:  \tag{OC.20}
346: \]
347: This evaluates the actual normal bundle; it does not assert that all
348: its lower-degree data vanish.
349: 
350: ## 5. The component line and the individual integers
351: 
352: F31--32 construct the page by the literal line subfan with rays
353: `(j,ceil(j/2),1)`, whose two source component degrees are `(-1,+1)`.
354: Its m separated norm shells embed because the displayed height
355: defects prevent reentry after the first zero. The positive radial
356: annular map commutes with the original contracting line lift and
357: retains its circle line. The finite root and its fixed scalar
358: character are also the actual ones of F29/F32. Consequently their
359: compact page and relative line have exactly
360: \[
361:  \chi(\mathcal O_{S_{\rm page}})=k,\qquad
362:  \sigma(S_{\rm page})=-8k,\qquad
363:  c_1(M)_{\rm rel}^2=-2k,
364:  \qquad p_{\rm rel}=-24k.
365:  \tag{OC.21}
366: \]
367: The `m` regular half-disc bands of F32 prove equality of the graph
368: meridian line with this actual line: their relative \(H^2\) vanishes, so
369: restriction is injective. The fixed `d` character is a base line on
370: the page; after `m` sheets its character is one, and an integral fibre
371: class change has square and pairing with `M` zero. All nonzero `I_2`
372: component degrees remain in the computation (OC.21).
373: 
374: The two cap markings are the literal unimodular map (OC.7); their
375: closed difference is `S1_u x S3`. The line and signature of that
376: difference are zero by the actual cap product maps in F31. Thus
377: the signed trace calculated in Sections 2--4 has
378: \[
379:  b^2=4c_1(M)_{\rm rel}^2=-8k,\qquad
380:  p_1(T\overline X)=-24k,
381: \]
382: with `pi_*x=+1` for both signs. The sphere-bundle pushforward equations
383: of K7, using the matched initial Cox product, give
384: \[
385:  k_{\rm Jin}=\frac{p_1(\overline\nu)+3b^2}{4}=-6k,
386:  \qquad
387:  \ell_{\rm Jin}=p_1(T\overline X)+p_1(\overline\nu)=-24k.
388:  \tag{OC.22}
389: \]
390: The exact relative coordinate morphism of K23 is
391: `m_rel=-ell/4`, `n_rel=-(4 k_Jin-ell)/4`,
392: `s=(4 k_Jin-ell)/24 mod28`. Hence (OC.1) follows. This calculation
393: uses the component line itself and does not infer the pair from the
394: already zero index.
395: 
396: ## 6. The three-curve reference and all actual fans
397: 
398: The simultaneous product (OC.5)--(OC.6) gives a delta-equivariant
399: representative of the physical return equal to the identity near
400: all three cusp curves. The period lattice is index one, so the
